# SYNOPSIS

```usage
sha256sum [-c] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`sha256sum` reads each <file> and writes a line for it: its SHA-256 digest as 64 lower-case hexadecimal digits, two spaces, and the <file>'s name.
With no <file> it reads standard input and names it `<stdin>`; a <file> of `-` is standard input too, and is also named `<stdin>`.

: `-c`
  Check digests instead.
  Each <file>, or standard input with none, is a list of lines as `sha256sum` writes them; for each line, `sha256sum` computes the digest of the file it names and writes the name followed by `: OK` or `: FAILED`.
  A digest and a name may also be separated by a space and `*`.
: `-b`, `-t`
  Accepted and ignored: every file is read as bytes.

`sha256sum` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/sha256sum`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/sha256sum`.
There is no native `sha256sum`.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace, with `abc` and no newline on standard input:

```text
% printf abc | sha256sum
ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad  <stdin>
```

